WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.128 --> 00:00:09.839
The year is 1656.

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:13.051
Your body is wracked 
by violent chills.

00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:17.096
Your head pounds,
your muscles are too weak to sit up,

00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:19.807
and you feel like rancid, hard-boiled eggs

00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:22.894
are squeezing out of your neck 
and armpits.

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:27.607
In your feverish state, you see 
a strange-looking man approach,

00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:33.071
his face obscured by a beak-like mask,
his body covered from head to toe.

00:00:33.571 --> 00:00:36.657
He examines you 
and even without seeing his face,

00:00:36.657 --> 00:00:38.493
you know the diagnosis:

00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:39.786
you have the plague.

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:44.373
The plague stands out as one of the most 
terrifying and destructive diseases

00:00:44.373 --> 00:00:45.833
in human history.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.838
It swept across large parts of Afro-
Eurasia in three separate pandemics

00:00:50.838 --> 00:00:54.967
starting in the 6th, 14th,
and 19th centuries;

00:00:55.176 --> 00:00:57.929
killed tens of millions of people,

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:02.683
and had— in the best of cases— 
about a 40% survival rate.

00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:08.689
The European plague doctor, with his
beaked mask and wizard-like robes,

00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:13.444
is one of the images most popularly
associated with plague today.

00:01:13.653 --> 00:01:17.990
He’s often found in books and films 
about the 14th century pandemic

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.909
known as the Black Death.

00:01:20.368 --> 00:01:23.579
The only problem is
that’s about as accurate as placing

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:28.209
a modern surgeon at the court
of Louis the 14th in Versailles.

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:30.711
The confusion is understandable though—

00:01:30.711 --> 00:01:33.297
the Black Death had several aftershocks,

00:01:33.297 --> 00:01:37.051
including a series of devastating
outbreaks in Western Europe

00:01:37.051 --> 00:01:39.303
during the 17th century.

00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:43.891
This is when the iconic plague doctor
actually emerged on the scene.

00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:47.812
First described in the early 17th century,

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:51.566
the outfit consisted of a hood 
with crystal eyepieces

00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:55.695
and a beak filled with a pungent 
combination of herbs and compounds.

00:01:56.028 --> 00:01:59.991
This could include cinnamon, pepper,
turpentine,

00:01:59.991 --> 00:02:03.661
roast copper, and powdered viper flesh.

00:02:03.744 --> 00:02:09.500
This recipe was inspired by the famed
2nd century Greco-Roman physician Galen,

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.462
and was thought to ward off poisoned air
known as miasma.

00:02:14.005 --> 00:02:18.759
People believed this bad air
spread plague after emanating from swamps

00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:20.052
and sources of decay,

00:02:20.052 --> 00:02:22.805
such as dead plants or animal carcasses.

00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:28.144
In earlier centuries, doctors across
Europe carried metal pomanders

00:02:28.144 --> 00:02:29.896
filled with similar mixtures,

00:02:30.062 --> 00:02:33.900
and it’s possible that the beak evolved
as a hands-free alternative.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:40.281
The rest of the costume, which included 
an oiled leather robe, boots and gloves,

00:02:40.281 --> 00:02:42.825
acted as kind of an early hazmat suit,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.163
likely designed to block miasma 
from entering through the skin’s pores.

00:02:47.371 --> 00:02:50.833
While this shows some basic understanding
that plague

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:53.169
spread from one place to another,

00:02:53.169 --> 00:02:55.963
these doctors couldn’t know that,
in most cases,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:00.051
the true culprit was a tiny flea 
transmitting the bacteria,

00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:03.846
Yersinia pestis, 
from one person or animal to another.

00:03:04.138 --> 00:03:07.183
It’s possible that the plague doctor’s
outfit may have provided

00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:10.102
some unintentional protection 
from flea bites.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:13.147
However, not enough information
survives to know whether

00:03:13.147 --> 00:03:18.277
the costumed doctors fared any better than
their ordinarily robed counterparts.

00:03:19.153 --> 00:03:23.574
It's no surprise that this bizarre getup
has captured popular imagination,

00:03:23.574 --> 00:03:28.537
despite the fact that its use was limited
to a few places in Italy and France

00:03:28.537 --> 00:03:31.624
during the 17th and early 18th centuries.

00:03:31.874 --> 00:03:35.670
Even at the time, it was viewed
with macabre fascination

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.257
and occasionally used to mock
the ineffective and corrupt

00:03:39.257 --> 00:03:41.717
practices of some physicians.

00:03:42.718 --> 00:03:47.265
Until the 20th century, there was 
no effective treatment for the plague,

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:51.644
but that didn’t stop doctors— 
costumed or not— from trying.

00:03:51.811 --> 00:03:55.064
They consulted the works of earlier
physicians for guidance,

00:03:55.314 --> 00:03:57.608
did what they could to fend off miasma,

00:03:57.608 --> 00:04:01.153
and prescribed a variety 
of concoctions and antidotes.

00:04:01.404 --> 00:04:05.116
They also relied on pre-modern
medical mainstays.

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:07.076
These could include bloodletting,

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:11.497
which involved draining (sometimes 
concerningly large amounts of) blood

00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:15.918
in an attempt to remove poison 
or restore the body’s natural balance.

00:04:16.294 --> 00:04:18.796
Or cupping, where the rim 
of a heated glass

00:04:18.796 --> 00:04:23.759
was placed over swollen lymph nodes 
in hopes of making them burst sooner—

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:28.222
a sign, when it occurred naturally,
that a plague patient was on the mend.

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:31.267
Or— perhaps most painfully—
cautery,

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:34.895
which involved lancing the lymph nodes
with a red-hot poker

00:04:34.895 --> 00:04:37.440
to release the blackened pus within.

00:04:38.357 --> 00:04:40.526
A lot has changed since their times.

00:04:40.609 --> 00:04:43.946
Modern medicine has given us the means
to quickly identify

00:04:43.946 --> 00:04:46.365
bacterial as well as viral threats

00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:48.534
and to effectively mobilize against them.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:52.163
We also have access to technologies
like test kits,

00:04:52.163 --> 00:04:56.250
masks to deter the spread 
of respiratory viruses, and vaccines;

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.920
and we conduct robust trials to make
sure they’re safe and effective.

00:05:00.212 --> 00:05:02.048
But some things don’t change:

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:05.968
we still depend on the courage
and compassion of medical professionals

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:08.179
who voluntarily risk their lives

00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:12.892
against an invisible attacker to help
and comfort those who need it most.

